    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having some trouble with an iam policy for an s3 bucket. i'm trying to let a lambda function write logs to it but i keep getting an \"access denied\" error in the cloudwatch logs even though i added the s3:PutObject permission. here is the policy i'm using:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-company-log-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\ncan u take a look and tell me what i'm missing? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The issue here is likely due to the trailing asterisk (`*`) in the `Resource` field. This allows the policy to be applied to all objects in the bucket, but doesn't specify the ` lambda currentState` permissions.\n\nTry removing the trailing asterisk, like this:\n\n```\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"logs:UploadLogFiles\",\n        \"logs:PutLogEvents\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:logs:*:*:*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHowever, for writing logs to an S3 bucket, you are more likely to be allowed the `logs:UploadLogFiles` and `logs:PutLogEvents` actions rather than `s3:PutObject`. \n\nIf you leave out the `lambda Estado` component, it could also cause an issue as AWS doesn't translate the lambda function's permissions directly to the iam policy statement.",
      "source": "target",
